pids from your  bloodstream and prevent storage  when you become more insulin resistant  fats  circulate in your blood and pump out  more insulin  an excess of glucose ends up being  converted to fatty acids  and being stored as fat in all the wrong  places  such as around your belly so in the long  term  this will lead to weight gain and even  health conditions like type 2 diabetes  we've spoken about how sleep affects  your food intake  unfortunately lack of sleep also have an  impact on your workouts  we want to increase lean muscle mass and  reduce body fat  having more lean muscle mass increases  your metabolism  helping you to burn fat more efficiently  this will also help you to maintain a  healthy body weight  long term however lack of sleep is  the enemy of our muscle sleep  deprivation can decrease  protein synthesis which is your body's  ability to increase  muscle it can also cause muscle loss and  lead to higher risk of injuries  i'm sure you have experienced this  yourself when you're tired and exhausted  you don't feel like working out even if  you do your training might not be at 100  everything you do feels much more  challenging and you struggle to give  your best effort lack of sleep will also  make it harder for your body to recover  that's because the production of growth  hormones that helps with fat burning and  also recovery  slows down in your body so when your  body can't  recover you can't progress in your  workout  most times people think that by spending  more hours exercising  it will speed up their results that's  absolutely  not true results come from getting  adequate time  in recovery because your muscle grows  and repairs when you rest  not when you work out so if you find  yourself hitting a plateau or not seeing  results  even though you're putting lots of  effort in your training  it can be due to your sleeping habits  and not prioritizing  your recovery  so now that you know the importance of  sleep and how it can help with wei